        "original_id": "e68918aa-2c61-54ba-b524-9835b5b1c369",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Luis Gianneo, Ariel Ramírez, Marta Lambertini, Juan Carlos Zorzi, Virtú Maragno, Pedro Ignacio Calderón, Rodolfo Arizaga, Roberto Caamano, Gerardo Gandini\n- Roles: composer, pianist, conductor, music educator, music director\n- Places: Argentina, San Isidro, Buenos Aires\n- Organizations: Universidad Catolica Argentina\n- Topics: Argentine folk music, music education\n\nFacts:\n- Luis Gianneo was an Argentine composer, pianist, conductor, and music educator who lived from 1897 to 1968.\n- Ariel Ramírez was born on 4 September 1921 and became an Argentine composer, pianist, and music director.\n- Luis Gianneo taught composers Ariel Ramírez, Juan Carlos Zorzi, Virtú Maragno, Pedro Ignacio Calderón, and Rodolfo Arizaga.\n- Ariel Ramírez was considered a chief exponent of Argentine folk music and was noted for iconic musical compositions.\n- Marta Lambertini was born in San Isidro, Buenos Aires.\n- Marta Lambertini studied at the Universidad Catolica Argentina with Roberto Caamano, Luis Gianneo, and Gerardo Gandini, and she graduated in 1972.\n- Ariel Ramírez died on 18 February 2010.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "15791477-73eb-50a6-8d77-806f3c87d6aa",
        "text": "Page: Luis Gianneo. Sentence: Luis Gianneo (1897–1968) was an Argentine composer, pianist and conductor.\nPage: Luis Gianneo. Sentence: As music educator, he was the teacher of composers Ariel Ramirez, Juan Carlos Zorzi, Virtú Maragno, Pedro Ignacio Calderón and Rodolfo Arizaga, among others.\nPage: Ariel Ramírez. Sentence: Ariel Ramírez (4 September 1921 – 18 February 2010) was an Argentine composer, pianist and music director.\nPage: Ariel Ramírez. Sentence: He was considered \"a chief exponent of Argentine folk music\" and noted for his \"iconic\" musical compositions.\nPage: Marta Lambertini. Sentence: She was born in San Isidro, Buenos Aires, and studied at the Universidad Catolica Argentina with Roberto Caamano, Luis Gianneo and Gerardo Gandini, graduating in 1972.",
